### Description

I have a certificate that is signed by Sectigo and one of the intermediary certs have an AIA entry of

```
Authority Information Access:
    CA Issuers - URI:http://crt.sectigo.com/SectigoPublicCodeSigningRootR46.p7c
    OCSP - URI:http://ocsp.sectigo.com
```

This URI is a PKCS 7 DER encoded file containing two certificates but the OpenSSL branch will call [OpenSslCertificateAssetDownloader.DownloadCertificate](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/2b185ac8c5c1302804a3336c62cb33782d9d4916/src/libraries/System.Security.Cryptography/src/System/Security/Cryptography/X509Certificates/OpenSslCertificateAssetDownloader.cs#L16-L40) to download the file at the AIA URI and then try and read the certificate with `new X509Certificate2(data)`. Unfortunately the `X509Certificate2` constructor is unable to open this certificate. it seems like OpenSSL does not support it altogether as it ends up failing on [OpenSslPkcsFormatReader.TryReadPkcs7](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/2b185ac8c5c1302804a3336c62cb33782d9d4916/src/libraries/System.Security.Cryptography/src/System/Security/Cryptography/X509Certificates/OpenSslPkcsFormatReader.cs#L220).

When the AIA traversal code encounters this exception it stops and considers the chain to be complete and fails with `PartialChain - unable to get local issuer certificate`.

### Reproduction Steps

A very simple reproducer of the problem at hand is.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

namespace AIATest;

public static class Program
{
    public static async Task Main(string[] args)
    {
        string downloadUri = "http://crt.sectigo.com/SectigoPublicCodeSigningRootR46.p7c";
        HttpClient client = new();
        HttpResponseMessage response = await client.GetAsync(downloadUri).ConfigureAwait(false);
        byte[] data = await response.Content.ReadAsByteArrayAsync().ConfigureAwait(false);

        // Fails here
        using X509Certificate2 cert = new(data);
    }
}
```

